  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Blackwell</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Blackwell is a family of NVIDIA AI chips. The NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 puts 72 Blackwell Ultra GPUs and 36 Grace CPUs in one rack. A GPU, or graphics processing unit, is the chip that does the AI math, and a CPU is the general-purpose processor that runs everything around it. <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/rubin/">Rubin</a> is the next NVIDIA family. Meta's purchase is on the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/meta/">Meta stack</a>.</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/blackwell.jpg" alt="NVIDIA Blackwell GPU package render: the compute silicon in the center with four memory stacks above and four below, inside a gold frame." caption="NVIDIA Blackwell GPU." credit="Image: NVIDIA" />
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">GB300 NVL72</h2>
    <p>NVIDIA announced the GB300 NVL72 on 18 March 2025. It is a rack-scale system, meaning the whole server rack is built as one machine. The rack connects 72 Blackwell Ultra GPUs and 36 Grace CPUs, which are based on Arm Neoverse designs. NVIDIA says the rack acts as a single GPU built for test-time scaling, which lets a model spend more computing power while it works out an answer. NVIDIA puts its AI performance at 1.5 times the GB200 NVL72 and said partner products would start in the second half of 2025. The product page also claims 1.5 times more dense FP4 Tensor Core FLOPS and 2 times higher attention performance than Blackwell GPUs. Attention is the step where a model weighs which earlier words matter. These are NVIDIA's own claims.</p>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
