    <p>On the product page, Tensor Core figures count sparsity unless a figure is marked without it. Sparsity is a shortcut that skips zeros in the math, so it reads higher. The second FP4 figure is the one without it. Tensor Cores are the parts of the GPU that do the matrix math behind AI. FP4 stores each number in 4 bits and FP8 in 8 bits. FP6, FP16, BF16, TF32, FP32, and FP64 are other number formats, and the larger ones are more precise but slower. INT8 stores whole numbers in 8 bits. A PFLOPS is a thousand trillion math operations per second and a TFLOPS is a trillion. POPS counts whole-number operations the same way. NVLink is NVIDIA's wiring that lets the GPUs share work. LPDDR5X is a low-power type of memory used by the CPUs, and ConnectX-8 is NVIDIA's network card.</p>

